  "body": "### What is the issue with the HTML Standard?\n\nIn the [report an exception](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/webappapis.html#report-an-exception) steps, we set `notHandled` to the result of firing the error event. So it will be false if the event was cancelled, or true otherwise. The steps later branch on this flag, where if the `notHandled` is false, \"the user agent may report exception to a developer console\".\n\nConsider the following two scripts:\n```html\n<script>\n    badreference;\n</script>\n```\n```html\n<script>\n    window.addEventListener(\"error\", (e) => {\n      e.preventDefault();\n    });\n    badreference;\n</script>\n\n```\n\nThese will both throw a `ReferenceError`. In the first script, we will have `notHandled` set to true, so we then will not enter the branch to report the exception to the developer console. In the second script, we cancel the error event, so `notHandled` is false and we then do enter that branch.\n\nIs that backwards? Chrome, Firefox, and Safari will all do the opposite - they will show the exception in the console for the first script, not the second script.\n\n",
